{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The Frank-Starling law states that within physiological limits, the more the heart muscle is stretched during filling, the greater the force of its contraction and the more blood it pumps into the aorta. End-diastolic volume is the preload. The law lets the heart automatically pump out whatever blood returns to it from the veins.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is the Frank-Starling law?

The **Frank-Starling law** (also called the Frank-Starling mechanism, Starling's law of the heart or the law of the heart) is an **intrinsic property of heart muscle**. Stretching the myocardial sarcomere (the contractile unit of muscle) improves the performance of the next contraction.

In simple words: **the more the heart fills, the harder it squeezes.** The law is stated in two common ways:

- The more the heart muscle is stretched during filling, the greater the force of contraction, and the greater the quantity of blood pumped into the aorta.
- Within physiological limits, the heart pumps all the blood that returns to it by way of the veins.

It is named after Otto Frank and Ernest Starling. Frank noticed that in muscle, the tension developed was related to length, and that changes in pressure in the heart came with matching changes in volume. Starling, using an isolated heart-lung preparation, observed that the mechanical energy released when muscle passes from rest to contraction depends on the length of the muscle fibre. He described this as the law of the heart, and said the heart follows the same rule as skeletal muscle.

## Key terms

- **Preload:** the degree to which the myocardium is stretched before it contracts. In the intact heart, the volume in the ventricles at the end of diastole stretches the resting muscle to a new length, so **end-diastolic volume is the preload**.
- **Afterload:** the resistance against which blood is expelled.
- **Stroke volume (SV):** the volume of blood ejected by a ventricle in one beat. It is the difference between end-diastolic volume and end-systolic volume.
- **Venous return:** the flow of blood back to the right atrium from the veins.
- **Contractility (inotropic state):** the work performed by cardiac muscle at any given end-diastolic fibre length.
- **Chronotropic action:** an effect on heart rate. **Inotropic action:** an effect on the strength of contraction.

## Why does stretch increase force? The length-tension relationship

The length-tension relationship in cardiac muscle is similar to that in skeletal muscle. As the muscle is stretched, the developed tension rises to a maximum and then falls if the stretch becomes extreme.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/frank-starling-law/fs-length-tension.webp" alt="Length-tension curve with sarcomere actin and myosin overlap." caption="Force depends on how much actin and myosin overlap." credit="TommyN94, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcomere_length-tension_relationship.svg" width="960" height="528" /%}

The explanation has two parts:

- **More cross-bridges.** Electron microscopy shows that sarcomere length, in the range of 2 to 2.2 micrometres, is positively related to the amount of actin and myosin cross-bridging. There is an optimal sarcomere length at which this interaction is greatest. A longer resting muscle forms more cross-bridges, so contraction is stronger.
- **Changes in calcium.** A stretched cardiac muscle develops greater tension because of changes in the calcium sensitivity of the myofibrils and in the amount of activator calcium.

Atrial and ventricular muscle both obey Starling's law. Increased initial fibre length (preload) increases resting tension, the velocity of tension development and the peak tension.

## The ventricular function curve

If you plot **stroke volume (or cardiac output) against ventricular end-diastolic volume (or pressure)**, you get the **Frank-Starling curve**, also called the ventricular function or Starling curve.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/frank-starling-law/fs-ventricular-function-curve.webp" alt="Frank-Starling curve of stroke volume against end-diastolic volume." caption="Greater filling gives greater stroke volume, up to a plateau." credit="Bbosramek, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frank-Starling_Law_and_Inotropy.png" width="960" height="611" /%}

Key features:

- In a healthy heart, as end-diastolic volume rises, cardiac output rises until a maximum is reached. After that, output cannot be raised further.
- Stretch raises output mainly at subnormal filling pressures. In the upright position, ventricular filling pressures fall (to about 4 mm Hg), and the normal heart operates on the **ascending limb** of the curve. Peak output occurs at filling pressures of about 10 mm Hg.
- The normal left ventricular function curve is affected much more by changes in **preload** than by other changes.
- The increased stroke volume gained by stretch also raises wall tension, and that **increases myocardial oxygen consumption**.

{% table %}
- **Situation**
- **Effect on the Frank-Starling relationship**
---
- **Positive inotropic factors** (exercise, adrenergic stimulation, digitalis)
- Move the heart to a different curve with higher contractility. For the same end-diastolic volume, output is higher
---
- **Compromised intrinsic contractility** (as in heart failure)
- A greater degree of dilation is needed to raise stroke volume, and the same maximal output as normal muscle is not reached
---
- **Chamber already dilated by raised preload** (left-to-right shunt, valvular insufficiency)
- Little room for further dilation to raise cardiac output
---
- **Increased afterload** (aortic or pulmonic stenosis, coarctation of the aorta)
- Cardiac performance falls and the relationship is depressed
{% /table %}

Each Frank-Starling curve corresponds to one level of contractility. Factors that change contractility therefore create a **family of Frank-Starling curves**. Contractility is changed by exercise, adrenergic stimulation, changes in pH, temperature and drugs such as digitalis.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Do not confuse the two ideas:** a change in preload moves the heart **along** one Frank-Starling curve. A change in contractility moves the heart to a **different** curve.
{% /callout %}

## Preload and afterload in the muscle strip model

In an isolated muscle strip, the **preload** is the load that stretches the muscle before it contracts, resting on a platform. The first phase of contraction is **isometric**: tension rises until it is enough to lift the load. The tension at which the load is lifted is the **afterload**. After that, the muscle shortens **isotonically** without further rise in tension.

In the living body, preload is how much the myocardium is stretched before contraction, and afterload is the resistance against which blood is expelled. The force of contraction depends on both.

## Venous return and cardiac output

Under most conditions, the amount of blood pumped by the heart each minute is determined almost entirely by the rate of blood flow into the heart from the veins, which is the **venous return**. Each peripheral tissue controls its own local blood flow. All of these local flows combine and return through the veins to the right atrium, and the heart then pumps this incoming blood into the arteries.

The factors in the peripheral circulation that affect venous return are normally the **primary controllers of cardiac output**. This works because of the Frank-Starling law: when more blood flows in, the walls of the heart chambers are stretched, the muscle contracts with greater force, and the extra blood is ejected without delay into the aorta.

There are two basic ways the pumping of the heart is regulated:

1. **Intrinsic regulation** in response to changes in the volume of blood flowing into the heart (the Frank-Starling mechanism)
2. **Control of heart rate and strength by the autonomic nervous system**

During strenuous exercise the heart may pump four to seven times the resting amount.

Stretch also acts on the **sinus node** in the wall of the right atrium. Stretching it has a direct effect on its rhythmicity and increases heart rate, so a rise in venous return gives a second boost to output through rate.

## What affects preload?

- **Heart rate.** Faster rates shorten diastole proportionately more than systole. Ventricular filling becomes progressively impaired at higher rates (above 120 beats per minute in adults).
- **Atrial contraction and rhythm.** Absent atrial contraction (atrial fibrillation), ineffective contraction (atrial flutter) or altered timing of atrial contraction (low atrial or junctional rhythms) can reduce ventricular filling by 20% to 30%. Patients whose ventricles are less compliant are more affected by losing a normally timed atrial systole.
- **Contractile state and disease**, including heart failure.
- **Excessive distension** of either ventricle can lead to excessive dilatation and incompetence of the atrioventricular valves.

**Measuring preload.** Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP) can stand in for preload only if the relationship between ventricular volume and pressure (compliance) is constant. In fact, compliance is normally nonlinear. Impaired diastolic function lowers compliance, so the same LVEDP that means a normal preload in a healthy person may mean a lower preload in someone with impaired diastolic function. In practice, pulmonary wedge pressure and central venous pressure are used as substitutes for ventricular volume. Direct measures of ventricular volume are also possible.

## Frank-Starling mechanism in heart failure

The Frank-Starling mechanism may remain intact even in a failing heart. In acute cardiac injury it helps to **maintain stroke volume**:

1. The injured ventricle ejects less, so **stroke volume falls**.
2. Blood left behind raises **left ventricular end-diastolic volume and pressure**.
3. By the Frank-Starling mechanism, this higher preload **increases the force of contraction** and helps restore stroke volume.

Together with neurohumoral activation (an extrinsic mechanism), this is an adaptation that comes into play within minutes of cardiac injury. The mechanism by which more stretch gives more force is also called the **law of heterometric autoregulation**.

In chronic heart failure, sodium retention, water retention and venoconstriction may be continuing attempts to use the same mechanism by raising left ventricular filling pressure and preload. Its compensatory role in the chronic setting is less certain.

**The cost.** These adaptations can lead to abnormally high pulmonary capillary and artery pressures, which probably contribute to the breathlessness of heart failure. Arterial constriction and stiffening from sodium and water retention raise afterload. The injured left ventricle is especially sensitive to afterload, so this can make it fail further. This sensitivity to afterload is called the **law of homeometric regulation**.

**Remodelling.** Ventricular remodelling after injury or in heart failure may change the Frank-Starling relationship.

## Overload and the heart's response

The heart tries to compensate for raised preload and raised afterload in several ways. One is ventricular hypertrophy, which tries to keep systolic wall stress in the normal range.

{% table %}
- **Type of overload**
- **Typical change**
- **At the tissue level**
---
- **Pressure overload** (for example aortic stenosis)
- Concentric hypertrophy
- Parallel replication of myofibrils and thickening of individual myocytes
---
- **Volume overload**
- Ventricular dilatation
- Replication of sarcomeres in series and elongation of myocytes
{% /table %}

In aortic stenosis, left ventricular pressure rises. The ventricle thickens its wall, and by Laplace's law, a thicker wall reduces wall stress even though pressure has risen. In a failing heart, the radius of the left ventricle increases, which increases wall stress.

These compensations seem able to maintain pump function only for a limited time.

## Clinical applications

- **Stroke volume and left ventricular end-diastolic volume:** this relationship is a common clinical use of Starling's law.
- **Exercise:** more venous return stretches the ventricles and increases output, and the sympathetic system adds to the effect.
- **Heart failure:** the failing ventricle works on a lower curve. It needs more dilation to raise stroke volume and cannot reach the same maximum.
- **Drugs:** adding an inotropic agent such as digoxin shifts the curve upward. For the same filling pressure, cardiac index rises.
- **Valve and shunt lesions:** a chamber already dilated by a left-to-right shunt or valvular insufficiency has little reserve to dilate further.
- **Obstructive lesions:** aortic or pulmonic stenosis and coarctation of the aorta raise afterload and depress the relationship.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Starling in one line:** "Fill more, squeeze harder, pump out what comes in." For heart failure: "Less contractility needs more dilation for the same output, and more afterload pushes the curve down."
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="In the intact heart, the volume in the ventricle at the end of diastole stretches the resting muscle to a new length. End-diastolic volume is therefore the preload." %}
In the intact heart, preload is best represented by:
- A. Aortic pressure
- B. End-diastolic volume
- C. End-systolic volume
- D. Heart rate
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Stretch increases the number of actin-myosin cross-bridges up to an optimal sarcomere length, and also alters the calcium sensitivity of the myofibrils and the amount of activator calcium." %}
The Frank-Starling law is explained mainly by:
- A. A fall in heart rate when the heart is stretched
- B. A rise in afterload during diastole
- C. More cross-bridge formation and changes in calcium handling when sarcomeres are stretched
- D. A fall in myocardial oxygen consumption
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Changes in contractility, such as exercise, adrenergic stimulation or digitalis, produce a different Frank-Starling curve. A change in preload only moves the heart along the same curve." %}
Digitalis shifts the heart to a different Frank-Starling curve because it:
- A. Increases contractility
- B. Increases preload only
- C. Decreases afterload only
- D. Decreases venous return
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Atrial fibrillation removes the normally timed atrial contraction and can reduce ventricular filling by 20% to 30%. Lower filling means lower preload." %}
Which of the following reduces ventricular filling and so reduces preload?
- A. Exercise
- B. A positive inotropic drug
- C. A normal sinus rhythm at rest
- D. Atrial fibrillation
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Aortic stenosis raises afterload, and increased afterload depresses the Frank-Starling relationship. The left ventricle responds with hypertrophy to keep wall stress normal." %}
A patient with aortic stenosis is most likely to show:
- A. A raised Frank-Starling curve because afterload is high
- B. A depressed Frank-Starling relationship with compensatory ventricular hypertrophy
- C. A fall in left ventricular wall thickness
- D. A fall in left ventricular pressure
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Within physiological limits the heart pumps all the blood that returns to it from the veins. Venous return is therefore the main controller of cardiac output, and the Frank-Starling mechanism makes the heart match its output to it." %}
Under normal conditions, cardiac output is controlled mainly by:
- A. The aortic valve
- B. The size of the atria
- C. Venous return, with the heart matching it by the Frank-Starling mechanism
- D. Coronary blood flow alone
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="State the Frank-Starling law." %}
Within physiological limits, the more the heart muscle is stretched during filling, the greater the force of contraction and the more blood is pumped into the aorta. The heart pumps all the blood that returns to it from the veins.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is preload in the intact heart?" %}
The stretch on the ventricular muscle before it contracts. It is represented by the end-diastolic volume.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the cellular basis of the law?" %}
Stretching the sarcomere towards its optimal length increases actin-myosin cross-bridging. Stretch also changes the calcium sensitivity of the myofibrils and the amount of activator calcium.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How does the Frank-Starling curve change with contractility?" %}
Each curve corresponds to one level of contractility. Exercise, adrenergic stimulation and digitalis move the heart to a higher curve. Heart failure moves it to a lower curve.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How does the law help in acute cardiac injury?" %}
Stroke volume falls, so end-diastolic volume and pressure rise. The raised preload increases the force of contraction and helps restore stroke volume.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the drawback of using the mechanism in chronic heart failure?" %}
Sodium and water retention and venoconstriction raise filling pressure and can cause high pulmonary capillary pressures and breathlessness. Rising afterload may also make the injured ventricle fail further.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How do pressure overload and volume overload differ in their effect on the ventricle?" %}
Pressure overload leads to concentric hypertrophy, with parallel replication of myofibrils. Volume overload leads to dilatation, with sarcomeres added in series and elongation of myocytes.
{% /viva %}

